cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, cite the software and the associated RCC paper."
title: "rcc-refcert: a Python research toolkit for structure-fair quantum circuit complexity"
type: software
version: 0.1.2
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22712591"
date-released: 2026-09-12
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
abstract: >-
  rcc-refcert is a Python research toolkit for structure-fair quantum circuit
  complexity. It implements finite-control models, reference certificates and
  conditional terminal-state bounds from Reference-Contingent Complexity (RCC),
  making supplied resources, allowed quantum processes, and description and
  generation costs explicit.
  Researchers can construct prefix-controlled quantum-process models, compute
  terminating program semidensities, and check Bellman-Choi, reference-potential,
  fixed-model domination, and reference gain-cost conditions. Complete exact
  spectra and predeclared fixed-projector counts provide conditional lower
  bounds on preparation cost in atomic slots. Freshly verified H.3/H.4 proof
  objects can supply the reference-domination constant; versioned records
  preserve calculation inputs, evidence levels and same-version scalar replay.
  Three bundled reference cases cover closed-form, multiblock, and nonhalting
  behavior. A worked paired-reset model connects the physical declaration to a
  numerical RCC lower
  bound of one slot, attained by an explicit one-slot preparation. The package
  provides a Python API, command-line tools, two Jupyter tutorials, and versioned
  reports. Matrix checks provide reproducible numerical evidence for declared
  finite models, with explicit pass, fail, inconclusive, and not-applicable
  outcomes. Terminal-state records distinguish scalar enclosures, numerical
  certificate evidence and sampling coverage. The accompanying paper supplies
  the physical and analytic premises of the general RCC framework.
keywords:
  - quantum circuit complexity
  - structure-fairness
  - reference-contingent complexity
  - quantum information
  - finite-control quantum processes
  - reference certificates
  - reproducible research
